  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how epigenetic marks are copied after DNA replication. In the top schematic, unmethylated cytosines are shown as light circles and methylated cytosines (5mC) as dark circles. DNMT3A and DNMT3B first establish DNA methylation on previously unmethylated DNA. After replication, each daughter double helix carries only part of the original methylation pattern, creating a hemimethylated state. DNMT1 then restores methylation on the new strand, preserving the original pattern across cell divisions.\n\nThe lower schematic shows an analogous process for histone methylation on nucleosomes. Before replication, an old nucleosome carries a methyl mark placed by a histone lysine methyltransferase (KMT). During replication, old nucleosomes are distributed onto daughter DNA together with newly assembled, unmodified nucleosomes. The inherited old nucleosomes appear to recruit or guide KMT activity, which then methylates neighboring new nucleosomes. This propagates the chromatin state after replication.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that both DNA methylation and histone methylation can be epigenetically inherited: pre-existing marks are diluted by replication but then copied back onto new DNA or histones to maintain cellular memory.",
